WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.219 --> 00:00:04.189
Hello everyone welcome to Paul and my name is the Lang focus channel

00:00:04.189 --> 00:00:05.909
several months ago

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:11.700
I released a video on the French language and more recently I released a video on cockney rhyming slang

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:17.309
And that got me thinking about french slang yes despite all the efforts of the French Academy

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:18.270
L'académie

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:24.989
Française to promote proper French or formal french french slang does exist and is commonly used

00:00:25.269 --> 00:00:32.159
One interesting type of French slang is called verlan that name verlan comes from the word l'envers

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.380
meaning backwards or inverse

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:42.270
The word syllables are reversed and we get verlan. The name of this type of slang contains an example of the slang

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:44.760
Verlan is a kind of metathesis

00:00:45.039 --> 00:00:52.589
Side by side sounds switching positions and in this type the basic idea is that two syllables in a word switch places with each other

00:00:53.110 --> 00:01:00.389
Note that this kind of slang originates in the spoken language so the spelling of the slang word comes from its sound rather than vice-versa

00:01:00.579 --> 00:01:06.239
the S at the end of l'envers is silent, that's why it's not part of the word verlan

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:12.240
I think you all know the standard French word Merci. In verlan, it's cimer.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.430
I'm sure you also know the word Bonjour. Can you guess what it would be in verlan?

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:22.679
Yes, it's jourbon. There's the word future. In verlan

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:28.499
It's turfu. Then there's the word Metro the metro or subway in verlan

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:35.189
it's tromé and the word bizarre meaning weird or bizarre in verlan it's zarbi.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:43.019
So where did this slang come from and who uses it? Verlan began to arise in the decades following World War two in the

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:46.259
poor suburbs of Paris since then it has entered mainstream

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:52.979
Culture through music TV movies and so on it's still used most heavily by young people in les banlieues

00:01:53.530 --> 00:02:01.439
Low-income suburbs of France's major cities where mostly immigrants and their descendants live but a certain amount of Vietnam is used by the wider

00:02:01.439 --> 00:02:07.169
mainstream population especially young people this sentence contains one of the most mainstream violent words

00:02:07.599 --> 00:02:15.299
Mon mec mate les autres meufs dans la rue. This means my boyfriend looks at other girls on the street the verlan word here is meuf

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:21.929
This is an inversion of the word femme, so it generally means woman or girl, but in the phrase ma meuf

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:26.400
It means my girlfriend as opposed to ma femme which means my wife

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.780
I guess the formality of the relationship is reflected in the formality of the word

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:32.999
The word mec is not verlan

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:39.570
But is also slang which means something like guy or dude and mon mec means my boyfriend in Paris though

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:41.440
You might hear people say keum

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:43.529
Which is a very long version of mec.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:51.540
But that one is less common than myth it might not be immediately obvious how these single syllable words become reversed at the end

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:53.160
of the word femme for instance

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.279
There is a release after the M consonant like a schwa

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:03.509
Femme that ma at the end becomes the first syllable in the reversed word and fact becomes the second syllable

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:09.929
But then the final vowel is dropped the schwa sound from gets spelled as EU and the vowel sound

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:11.560
naturally changes a little bit

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:18.989
This is how most single syllable verlan words are formed a few more examples. Mère meaning mother becomes reum

00:03:19.269 --> 00:03:22.979
J'habite chez ma reum. This sentence means: I live at my mom's place

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:29.040
Père meaning father becomes reup. Fête meaning party becomes teuf

00:03:29.799 --> 00:03:31.290
On fait une teuf à la sonmai?

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:34.649
Are we having a party at home in this sentence contains

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:40.410
One more verlan word sonmai which is a reversal of maison, the word meaning house

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:46.049
Sometimes not just a vowel but a consonant might disappear if it's awkward to pronounce

00:03:46.239 --> 00:03:52.109
There's the word flic which means cop like a police officer and the verlan word is keuf

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:56.850
You might be expecting it to sound like could flee but the final syllable gets dropped

00:03:56.850 --> 00:04:02.399
And it's just keuf. Attention v'là les keufs! this means watch out the cops are here

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:06.030
V'là by the way is an abbreviated form of voilà

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:11.880
Another common change that happens, is that a sound is removed before the inversion takes place

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:15.869
So we have the word legal a meaning to laughs which becomes

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:16.890
golri

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:24.689
So the ER ending was removed from the word before reversing the two syllables the innovators of slang normally stay one step ahead of the

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:30.969
General population and when a word or expression becomes two common they often stop using it in the case of verlan

00:04:31.129 --> 00:04:32.569
some words have become

00:04:32.569 --> 00:04:39.819
re-verlanized or like double verlan before I mentioned the word flic which became verlanized to keuf

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:44.770
But that word has become very well-known so people verlanized it again to feuk

00:04:45.039 --> 00:04:46.240
Maybe it was re-verlanized

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:52.689
because the police started to understand the word keuf and people wanted to talk about the police without them understanding

00:04:53.060 --> 00:05:00.339
Another example the verlan version of the word a arabe meaning Arab is beur and this is one of the most well known

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:06.489
verlan words it became re-verlanized as rebeu and meuf from family woman

00:05:07.039 --> 00:05:13.149
sometimes gets re-verlanized to feumeu. The use of verlan is not constant or consistent

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:19.299
It's usually limited to a couple of words in each sentence and those same words are not always used in their slang form

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:20.050
also

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:26.650
when verbs are verlanized they can't be conjugated so verbs are only used in these forms the infinitive the

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:32.379
Present tense the imperative and also the past participle so for example for the word

00:05:32.689 --> 00:05:37.539
golri from rigoler - laughs you can say je golri meaning, I laugh

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:43.689
J'ai golri meaning I laughed with this functioning as the past participle or je vais golri

00:05:43.689 --> 00:05:49.809
I'm going to laugh with this being the infinitive form you could also use the imperative form Golri!

00:05:49.939 --> 00:05:55.359
But the meaning of that imperative form would be kind of strange for this verb. For any of the other conjugations

00:05:55.360 --> 00:06:01.960
you need to use the standard verb rigoler and conjugate it for example for the conditional present form you would use

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:07.509
rigolerais. So verlan is somewhat limited in when it can be used now

00:06:07.509 --> 00:06:13.989
Let's check out a few examples of verlan in context these examples all come from the French film called La Haine.

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:16.899
This movie takes place in one of the lower income

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:24.759
suburbs of Paris where most of the population are of immigrant background. The film contains numerous examples of verlan here's the first clip

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:38.059
Ta gueule. Téma la vache, téma la vache. The guy in the clip says Téma la vache.

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:42.380
Which means look at the cow this word téma is the verlan word for

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:46.309
mater a verb which we saw earlier in this sentence mon mec

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:50.330
mate les autres meufs dans la rue. Now let's see that one more time a little slower

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:56.059
version here's one more clip with the same word

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:06.980
This time someone says Téma le virage meaning watch the turn now, let's see that one more time

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:16.729
Here's another clip with a different example

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:31.580
In this clip the guy says Je suis le démon de la turevoi! meaning

00:07:31.580 --> 00:07:34.999
I'm the demon of the car and the scene they're about to steal a car

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:39.739
The verlan word is turevoi which comes from voiture - meaning car

00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:46.309
He actually pronounces the second syllable in a more casual way saying something like "turwee" of way instead of "turevoi"?

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:48.550
Now let's see that one more time a little slower

00:07:57.310 --> 00:08:01.709
And one more example "Va te coucher, l'relou"

00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:05.969
The guy in the clip says "Va te coucher, l'relou"

00:08:05.969 --> 00:08:13.589
Which means go to sleep annoying guy or something like that the word relou comes from the word lourd which means heavy but

00:08:13.719 --> 00:08:19.139
relou means annoying or irritating. Now let's see that one more time, Va te coucher, l'relou.

00:08:21.009 --> 00:08:24.628
Verlan is a dynamic form of slang meaning that it's always changing

00:08:24.789 --> 00:08:30.149
And old words might go out of use and new words might arise or words might become re-verlanized

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:33.630
It's a kind of casual speech that you won't find in textbooks

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:39.539
And you might find confusing when you first encounter it you can learn some verlan online or from books

00:08:39.539 --> 00:08:43.018
But you might sound funny if you overdo it in the wrong context

00:08:43.019 --> 00:08:48.809
So it's probably best to learn it in context when speaking with native speakers but for our purposes here today

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:50.810
It's simply an interesting linguistic

